501:
All businesses operating as an amusement game room hereunder shall be subject to the following regulations:
(1) Devices to be kept in plain view; gambling devices prohibited. All machines shall at all times be kept and placed in plain view of and open and accessible to any person(s) who may frequent or be in any place of business where such machines are kept or used. Nothing in this section shall be construed to authorize, permit or license any gambling device of any nature whatsoever.
(2) Inspection. The chief of police shall inspect or cause the inspection of any place or building in which any such machine(s) are operated or set up for operating, and shall inspect, investigate and test such machines as needed.
(3) Attendant required. It shall be unlawful for any proprietor to open his business to the public unless an attendant is present. Said attendant shall be of sufficient mental and physical capacity so as to be able to provide aid to patrons if needed or desired. Said attendant shall not be less than 18 years of age.
(4) Loitering. As used in this section, "loitering" shall mean remaining idle in essentially one location and shall include the concepts of spending time idly, loafing or walking about aimlessly, and shall be unlawful for any person, firm or corporation licenses to operate an amusement game room to permit loitering on or in the immediate vicinity of any machine or business premises regulated hereunder in such a manner as to:
a. Create or cause to be created a danger of a breach of the peace;
b. Create or cause to be created any disturbance or annoyance to the comfort and repose of any person;
c. Obstruct the free passage of pedestrians or vehicles;
d. Obstruct, molest or interfere with any person lawfully in a public place;
e. Shirt and shoes required. All proprietors shall require shirts and shoes to be worn at all times by any person frequenting their premises.
(Ord. No. 11-13-01, § 2, 12-3-2013)

514:
(a) All persons desiring to transact business in the city as a transient merchant, itinerant merchant, itinerant vendor, or itinerant vendor of prepared foods under the provisions of this article shall submit to a criminal background check and file a written business registration, verified by affidavit and signed by the registrant, if an individual, by all partners, if a partnership, or by the president, if a corporation, with the secretary of the city commission, showing:
(1) The name, date of birth, driver's license number, social security number, and description of the person who will conduct the registrant's business during the time that it is proposed to be carried on in the city, the name, date of birth, driver's license number, and social security number of all other persons who will participate in the transaction of business for the registrant in the city, the local address of such persons while engaged in such business, the permanent address of such persons, the fingerprints of such persons, the capacity in which such persons will act (that is, whether as proprietor, agent or otherwise), the name and address of the person for whose account the business will be carried on, if any, and if a corporation, the state in which it was incorporated;
(2) The place in the city where such person proposes to conduct the registrant's business, and the length of time for which it is proposed that such business be conducted;
(3) A statement of the nature, character, and quality of the goods, wares, or merchandise to be sold or offered for sale by the registrant in the city; whether the same are proposed to be sold from stock in possession or from stock in possession and by sample, at auction, by direct sale or by taking orders for future delivery; and where the goods or the goods or property proposed to be sold are located at the time such registration is filed;
(4) If the registrant is not the individual owner of the business registered, the credentials of the person for which the registrant proposes to do business, authorizing the registrant to act as such representative;
(5) The length of time for which the right to do business is desired; and
(6) A photograph of the registrant, showing his/her facial features as they appear at the time such registration is filed, which shall be a two-inch by two-inch picture showing the head and shoulders of the registrant in a clear and distinguishing manner.
(b) Any person desiring to transact business in the city as a peddler, canvasser, solicitor, or solicitor of subscriptions to publications under the provisions of this article shall submit to a criminal background check and file a written business registration, signed by the registrant and verified by his/her affidavit, with the secretary of the city commission, showing the following:
(1) The name, date of birth, driver's license number, social security number, fingerprints, and description of the registrant;
(2) A photograph of the registrant, showing his/her facial features as they appear at the time such registration is filed, which shall be a two-inch by two-inch picture showing the head and shoulders of the registrant in a clear and distinguishing manner;
(3) Permanent home address and full local address of the registrant;
(4) A brief description of the nature of the business and the goods or services to be sold, or the publications for which subscriptions will be solicited;
(5) If employed, the name and address of the employer, together with credentials establishing the exact relationship; and
(6) The length of time for which the right to do business is desired.
(c) The city, after authorization to initiate a criminal background check by the applicant, shall complete such criminal background check within three business days of the date of written registration.
(Prior Code, § 29-9)

